  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday and Wednesday: An upper level low sliding through northern \nCalifornia today will bring thickening clouds to the Inland NW a \nsmall chance of sprinkles to central Washington in the morning. \nClouds will begin to clear out in the evening as the system departs \ninto Nevada, leaving Wednesday dry and mild. Temperatures Tuesday \nand Wednesday will be in the upper 40s to 50s. \n\nThursday through Tuesday: Another system comes hot on its heels \nearly Thursday into the Pacific NW. Snow levels will be around 4000-\n5000 feet in the Cascades early Thursday, but favorable synoptic \nascent is short lived as the decaying shortwave trough races through \nthe region and departs in the afternoon Thursday. There is a 20 \npercent chance of at least 1 inch of snow at Washington Pass \nThursday morning. This will also bring light rain to eastern WA and \nnorth ID in the morning and afternoon, but amounts look very \nunimpressive around a trace to a tenth of an inch. \n\nAnother shortwave will brush through the region Friday and this \nsystem will have more moisture to work with but the best synoptic \nascent is displaced to the north in Canada. Strong westerly flow \nwill maximize preciptiation near the Cascade crest where a tenth to \nhalf an inch of precipition is forecast Friday and Friday night. \nSnow levels will start off around 4000-4500 feet in the morning \nbringing snow to both Stevens and Washington Pass but rise above \n5000 feet in the afternoon. The chance for at least 1 inch of snow \nat Stevens Pass from Friday morning to Friday night is 10%. Those \nchances increase to 45% for Washington Pass. We will take anything \nwe can get. Outside of the Cascades, precipitation amounts will be \nlight around a trace to a few hundreths with this system. \n\nUnsettled conditions continue into the weekend primarily in the \nmountains. Global ensembles are coming in better agreement that the \nPacific NW will be on the edge of high pressure to the east and low \npressure to the west. Snow levels at least initially appear to high \nto get snow at the Cascade Passes Saturday but there is a 25% chance \nof at least 6 inches of snow at Washington Pass Sunday and Monday. A \nsimilar pattern appears to set up into next week with the longwave \ntrough remaining anchored in the northeast Pacific. /DB&&",
